<header class="ref-head"><span class="hero-ornament"></span><h1 class="research-title">7 March in history</h1><p class="ref-lede">9 events that happened on 7 March, from 161 to 1965.</p></header>
<ol class="day-events"><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">161 CE <span class="tih-ago">1,865 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Marcus Aurelius becomes Roman Emperor</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">On the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antoninus-pius">Antoninus Pius</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcus-aurelius">Marcus Aurelius</a> succeeded and made his adoptive brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lucius-verus">Lucius Verus</a> co-emperor. A Stoic philosopher, he wrote his 'Meditations' while campaigning on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a>.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">321 CE <span class="tih-ago">1,705 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Constantine I makes Sunday the Roman day of rest</h2><p class="tih-event-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantine-i">Emperor Constantine I</a> decreed that judges, towns and all trades rest on the day of the sun, making Sunday the Roman day of rest. The day honoured Sol Invictus, the official sun god of the late empire.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantine-i">Constantine I</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1138">1138 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">888 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Conrad III is elected King of Germany at Coblenz</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Conrad III, a former anti-king against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lothair-i">Lothair</a> III, was elected King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> at Coblenz in the presence of the papal legate Theodwin, becoming the first ruler of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hohenstaufen">Hohenstaufen dynasty</a>. His election began a long rivalry with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-welf">Welfs</a>.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/conrad-iii-of-germany">Conrad III of Germany</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1799">1799 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">227 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Napoleon storms Jaffa</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">French troops took the port of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jaffa">Jaffa</a> during <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>'s Syrian campaign. Thousands of prisoners were then executed, and plague broke out in the army, which was halted at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acre">Acre</a>.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1850">1850 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">176 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Daniel Webster's 'Seventh of March' speech backs compromise</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">The Massachusetts senator supported Henry Clay's compromise proposals, including a tougher fugitive slave law, to preserve the Union. Many Northern abolitionists denounced him.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card tih-has-thumb tih-event-hero"><p class="tih-hero-kicker">&#10022; Anniversary of the day</p><a class="tih-event-thumb" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-graham-bell" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/alexander-graham-bell.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="168" height="112"></a><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1876">1876 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">150 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Alexander Graham Bell is granted his telephone patent</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">US Patent 174,465 covered a method of transmitting speech electrically. It became one of the most valuable patents ever issued and was defended in hundreds of lawsuits.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag tih-tag-profile" href="/empire/united-states">United States</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-graham-bell">Alexander Graham Bell</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1936">1936 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">90 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">German troops reoccupy the Rhineland</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Hitler sent soldiers into the demilitarised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhineland">Rhineland</a> in breach of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/versailles">Versailles</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/locarno">Locarno</a> treaties. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> did not respond with force, encouraging further German expansion.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/versailles">Versailles</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1945">1945 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">81 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">US troops seize the Remagen bridge over the Rhine</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">American soldiers captured the Ludendorff railway bridge at Remagen before German demolition charges could destroy it. It gave the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a> their first crossing of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a>.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1965">1965 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">61 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">'Bloody Sunday' on the Edmund Pettus Bridge in Selma</h2><p class="tih-event-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alabama">Alabama</a> state troopers attacked about 600 civil rights marchers with clubs and tear gas. Televised images shocked the nation and helped secure the Voting Rights Act that August.</p></div></li></ol>
